{"text": " Traditional media finds whatever gets the most clicks and runs it into the ground until a new story they can sell comes along. We've been trained to expect financial risk to arrive as a single event, like a bubble popping or a headline. So a third of the developed world's debt is about to get swapped out, one maturity at a time, for the same debt at a much higher price. And we're already seeing it happen. One fifth of the fixed rate bonds outstanding in 2025 have been issued at a yield of more than 4%. The highest rate since 2015. That's a third of the developed world's fixed rate debt maturing by 2027, not a forecast, a slow renewal from cheap debt to expensive debt at about 2 percentage points higher. Nothing has to go wrong for that to happen. The bonds just have to reach their maturity date. The supply of paper is exploding, while demand for it thins at the long end, which results in the cost of borrowing going up for everyone at the same time. And while the long end continues to get more expensive, as the demand doesn't match the supply, everyone moves short, and none of what's happening is hidden. The data is sitting right in front of our eyes. You just have to know where to look. Nobody takes the time to connect the dots and dig deeper anymore.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.0, "text": " Traditional media finds whatever gets the most clicks and runs it into the ground until", "tokens": [50363, 29065, 2056, 7228, 4232, 3011, 262, 749, 25785, 290, 4539, 340, 656, 262, 2323, 1566, 50563], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13402370845570283, "compression_ratio": 1.5952380952380953, "no_speech_prob": 0.0018119366141036153, "words": [{"word": " Traditional", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.34, "probability": 0.45787525177001953}, {"word": " media", "start": 0.34, "end": 0.74, "probability": 0.9667565822601318}, {"word": " finds", "start": 0.74, "end": 1.1, "probability": 0.967431902885437}, {"word": " whatever", "start": 1.1, "end"